# HomeProxy fork

This repository is a personal fork of
[ImmortalWrt HomeProxy](https://github.com/immortalwrt/homeproxy). It keeps the
upstream LuCI application and adds a routing fix for custom domain rules, plus
an APK-only CI output and trusted package feed for apk-based OpenWrt systems.
The upstream OpenWrt package Makefile remains available; only this fork's
standalone GitHub workflow is APK-only.

The fork has been tested with OpenWrt 25.12, `apk-tools` 3.0.5 and sing-box
1.12.25. The HomeProxy package itself is `noarch`; its runtime dependencies are
still supplied by the configured OpenWrt or ImmortalWrt repositories.

## Differences from upstream

### Reverse DNS mapping for custom routing

Upstream HomeProxy can route a DNS request through a selected outbound while a
subsequent connection to the resolved IP address falls through to the default
route. This is visible with rules such as `dns_rule_outside_gb`: DNS identifies
the domain correctly, but an application that connects using only the returned
IP address may bypass the intended VPN route.

This fork adds **Reverse DNS mapping** under **HomeProxy → DNS Settings**. When
enabled in custom routing mode, sing-box records the domain-to-IP association
from a DNS answer and can attach that domain to a later IP-only connection. The
generated configuration then:

1. enables `dns.reverse_mapping`;
2. evaluates the custom route rules using the reverse-mapped domain;
3. performs a non-final route-level protocol sniff when no custom route rule
   has matched;
4. evaluates the custom route rules again using any domain discovered by
   sniffing.

Inbound legacy sniffing is disabled only while the Reverse DNS mapping option
is active in custom routing mode. This avoids an empty TLS SNI overwriting a
domain recovered from DNS, while retaining SNI and HTTP Host routing for
connections that have no reverse-DNS entry.

The option defaults to disabled, preserving upstream behaviour until it is
explicitly enabled.

### Signed APK feed

GitHub Actions builds an APKv3 package and, for pushes to `dev` or published
releases whose commit is contained in `dev`, signs both the package and its
`packages.adb` index. The signed feed is published with GitHub Pages:

```text
https://hermanho.github.io/homeproxy/packages.adb
```

The `master` branch is reserved as an upstream mirror. Pushes to `master` and
pull requests produce unsigned artifacts and never receive the production
signing secret. The `dev` branch contains this fork's published changes.
Production signing is isolated in the `feed-production` GitHub Environment.
The private signing key is not stored in this repository.

### ImmortalWrt-style package versions

APK versions use the same date/revision shape as LuCI packages built by
ImmortalWrt:

```text
YY.JJJ.SSSSS~COMMIT
```

For example, `26.236.50544~cb5d434` represents year 2026, UTC day 236,
50,544 seconds after UTC midnight, and Git commit `cb5d434`. The timestamp and
hash come from the latest commit affecting the packaged sources, including the
translations bundled by this fork. The tracked paths are `htdocs/`, `root/`,
`po/`, `Makefile` and `.github/build-pkg.sh`. Rebuilding the same clean commit
therefore keeps the same package version. A local build from a dirty worktree
can contain uncommitted changes without changing this version; commit all
package changes before distributing a build.

## Install from the trusted feed

The public signing key is intentionally public. Its SHA-256 fingerprint is:

```text
fb625db9f36e116cc3b873e0ebe2e1e79d583b1d8863ffeeb65e76a7d32b751d
```

The initial trust decision still depends on trusting this repository. For a
stronger bootstrap, record or verify the fingerprint through an independent
channel before installing the key.

Run the following as `root` on the router. It checks required commands, enforces
the fingerprint and refuses to replace a different key with the same filename:

```sh
set -eu

for cmd in apk awk chmod cp grep mkdir mktemp rm sha256sum touch wget; do
  command -v "$cmd" >/dev/null || {
    echo "Missing required command: $cmd" >&2
    exit 1
  }
done

EXPECTED_KEY_SHA256='fb625db9f36e116cc3b873e0ebe2e1e79d583b1d8863ffeeb65e76a7d32b751d'
KEY_FILE='/etc/apk/keys/homeproxy-feed.pem'
KEY_DOWNLOAD="$(mktemp /tmp/homeproxy-feed.XXXXXX)"
trap 'rm -f "$KEY_DOWNLOAD"' EXIT

wget -O "$KEY_DOWNLOAD" \
  https://hermanho.github.io/homeproxy/homeproxy-feed.pem

DOWNLOADED_KEY_SHA256="$(sha256sum "$KEY_DOWNLOAD" | awk '{print $1}')"
[ "$DOWNLOADED_KEY_SHA256" = "$EXPECTED_KEY_SHA256" ] || {
  echo "HomeProxy feed key fingerprint mismatch" >&2
  exit 1
}

if [ -e "$KEY_FILE" ]; then
  INSTALLED_KEY_SHA256="$(sha256sum "$KEY_FILE" | awk '{print $1}')"
  [ "$INSTALLED_KEY_SHA256" = "$EXPECTED_KEY_SHA256" ] || {
    echo "Refusing to overwrite a different $KEY_FILE" >&2
    exit 1
  }
fi

mkdir -p /etc/apk/keys /etc/apk/repositories.d
cp "$KEY_DOWNLOAD" "$KEY_FILE"
chmod 0644 "$KEY_FILE"
touch /etc/apk/repositories.d/customfeeds.list

FEED='https://hermanho.github.io/homeproxy/packages.adb'
grep -qxF "$FEED" /etc/apk/repositories.d/customfeeds.list || \
  echo "$FEED" >> /etc/apk/repositories.d/customfeeds.list

apk update
apk policy luci-app-homeproxy
apk add luci-app-homeproxy
```

Do not use `--allow-untrusted`. If signature verification fails, stop and check
the downloaded public-key fingerprint and feed URL rather than bypassing
verification.

To install future builds:

```sh
apk update
apk upgrade luci-app-homeproxy
```

## Build an APK locally

Clone the repository with full Git history and run the build from its root. On
a Linux build host, this requires Bash, Git, APKv3 `apk`, `fakeroot`, `po2lmo`,
GNU coreutils and GNU find:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/hermanho/homeproxy.git
cd homeproxy
fakeroot bash .github/build-pkg.sh apk
```

The APK is written to `.github/` as:

```text
luci-app-homeproxy-YY.JJJ.SSSSS~COMMIT.apk
```

Local builds are unsigned unless they are subsequently signed with the private
feed key. The GitHub Actions workflow is the supported path for producing and
publishing trusted packages. Use a clean, committed worktree for any package
that will be distributed.

## Development notes

The following upstream-oriented work remains open:

- Improve subscription-page response time with a large number of nodes.
- Refactor nftables rules.
- Move ACL settings to a dedicated page.
- Continue tracking upstream HomeProxy and sing-box configuration changes.
